entries.body:
A framework providing a way to program scripts securely on Bitcoin. Bitcoin's native language is called script. It is quite complex to use in practice, notably for sophisticated and customized applications. Above all, it is very difficult to verify the limitations of a script. Miniscript uses a subset of Bitcoin scripts to simplify their creation, analysis and verification. Each miniscript is equivalent 1 for 1 to a native script. An easy-to-use *policies* language is used, which is then compiled into miniscript, to finally correspond to a native script. 

![Miniscript hierarchy: a policy compiles into miniscript, equivalent 1 to 1 to a native Bitcoin script.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoicPandul/Dictionnaire-de-Bitcoin/bab568e32351cc9f1240f49f5feedc242123d101/definitions/fr/m/miniscript/assets/image-1.png)

Miniscript thus allows developers to build sophisticated scripts in a safer and more reliable way. The essential properties of Miniscript are as follows: 
* It allows static analysis of the script, notably of the spending conditions it allows and of its cost in terms of resources;
* It makes it possible to produce scripts that comply with consensus;
* It makes it possible to analyze whether or not the different spending paths comply with the standard rules of nodes;
* It makes it possible to establish a general, understandable and composable standard for all wallet software and hardware.

The Miniscript project was launched in 2018 by Pieter Wuille, Andrew Poelstra and Sanket Kanjalkar, through the company Blockstream. Miniscript was added to the Bitcoin Core wallet in *watch-only* mode in December 2022 with version 24.0, then fully in May 2023 with version 25.0.
